Docker Compose flags fall into two categories: 
- global flags that apply to any subcommand (like -f or -p)
- command-specific flags that only work with a particular subcommand (like up --build or down -v).

= Global Flags (apply to any docker compose command)
These come before the subcommand, e.g. docker compose -f custom.yaml -p myproj up.

Flag	                    What it does	Support relevance
-f, --file <path>      Specifies the Compose file to use. Can be repeated to merge multiple files (later files override earlier ones).  
  Also supports oci:// registry artifacts and git URLs. -- USECASES: Critical when a project uses non-default filenames or override files (docker-compose.override.yml). 
    If a user says "my changes aren't taking effect," check which file is actually being loaded.
-p, --project-name <name>	    Sets the project name, which prefixes all containers, networks, and volumes.	-- USECASES: If two projects have the same service names, 
  they collide unless project names differ. This is why docker compose down in one directory doesn't affect another.
--profile <name>	        Enables optional services tagged with that profile in the YAML .	  
     --USECASES: Some services (e.g., debug tools, monitoring) are only started when their profile is active. "Why isn't service X running?" → check its profile.
--parallel <n>	       Limits concurrent engine calls/builds .	--USECASES: Rarely needed in support, but useful when builds overwhelm a machine.
--dry-run	             Shows what would happen without executing.	Safe way to preview a destructive command.

= Command Specific flags
* docker compose up — Create and Start
The most-used command. Flags control whether to rebuild, run detached, and how to handle dependencies.

Flag	                           What it does
-d, --detach	            Runs containers in the background and returns the terminal .
--build	                  Forces a rebuild of images before starting, even if a cached image exists . Use after changing a Dockerfile or source code.
--no-build	              Never build; fail if the image doesn't exist locally .
--force-recreate	        Recreates containers even if their configuration hasn't changed . Useful when a container is in a bad state.
--no-recreate	            If a container already exists, don't recreate it .
--no-deps                 Don't start linked/dependency services . Useful when you only want one service and its dependencies are already running.
--remove-orphans     	    Removes containers for services no longer defined in the Compose file . Fixes "stale container" issues after editing the YAML.
--pull <policy>	          always, policy, or never — controls whether to pull newer images .
-V, --renew-anon-volumes	Recreates anonymous volumes instead of reusing them .

Support scenario: User edits a service's environment in the YAML and runs docker compose up -d, but nothing changes.
Cause: Compose reuses the existing container. Fix: docker compose up -d --force-recreate <service>.

* docker compose down — Stop and Remove
Tears down what up created. The flags control how much gets deleted.

Flag	                                            What it does
-v, --volumes	                          Removes named volumes declared in the volumes: section, plus anonymous volumes attached to containers .
--rmi <type>	                          Removes images: local (only untagged images) or all (all images used by services) .
--remove-orphans	                      Removes containers for services not in the Compose file .
-t, --timeout <seconds>	                Shutdown timeout before SIGKILL (default 10s) .

Critical warning for support: docker compose down -v deletes data. Named volumes survive a plain down, but -v removes them. If a user reports 
"my database is empty after restart," ask whether they ran down -v or down --volumes. Also note: external networks and volumes are never removed by down.

* docker compose build — Build or Rebuild Images
Flag	                                What it does
--no-cache	                   Disables the build cache; forces a full rebuild from source .
--pull	                       Always attempts to pull a newer base image .
--build-arg <key=value>	       Passes a build-time variable (same as Dockerfile ARG) .
--parallel	                   Builds images in parallel .
-q, --quiet	                   Suppresses build output .
--push	                       Pushes service images after building .

Support scenario: A Dockerfile change isn't reflected in the running container. docker compose up --build (or docker compose build --no-cache then up)
forces the image to be regenerated.

* docker compose run — One-off Tasks
Different from up: it runs a single service for an ad-hoc command (tests, migrations, debugging) and does not start the full stack by default.

Flag	                 What it does
--rm	                       Removes the container after the command exits. Essential for one-off tasks so you don't accumulate dead containers.
--no-deps	                   Doesn't start dependency services.
-e, --env <key=value>	       Sets an environment variable for this run only.
-T                          	Disables pseudo-TTY allocation (useful in scripts/CI).
Key distinction from the Docker FAQ: up starts the whole project; run is for "one-off or adhoc tasks" and only starts dependencies of the named service .

Quick Reference: Which Flag for Which Symptom
Symptom	Likely                                                 flag fix
"I changed the YAML but nothing changed"	                    up --force-recreate
"I changed the Dockerfile but the container is old"	          up --build
"My database is empty after restart"	                        Check for down -v — that's the cause
"Stale containers from removed services"	                    down --remove-orphans or up --remove-orphans
"Service isn't starting"                                 	    Check --profile — it may be optional
"Wrong Compose file is being used"                          	-f <path> to specify explicitly
"Two projects are colliding"	                                -p <unique-name>


--scale is used to specify the number of container instances to run for a service, and it has two main usage scenarios in Docker Compose:
as a flag for docker compose up / create, or as an independent docker compose scale command.

Two Usage Methods
1. As a flag for up or create
Use --scale directly when starting services, in the format service=number:
* docker compose up -d --scale backend=3
This will start 3 container instances for the backend service. This flag overrides the scale setting in the Compose file.

2. As an independent command
Use docker compose scale to adjust the number of instances for already running services:

* docker compose scale backend=3 frontend=2
The usage is docker compose scale [SERVICE=REPLICAS...], supporting scaling of multiple services simultaneously.

Key Restrictions (Important for Support)
Cannot scale services with container_name specified. If a service in your Compose file has a fixed container_name (e.g., container_name: my-backend),
Docker cannot create multiple containers with the same name. When attempting to scale, Docker will report a name conflict error.
To use --scale, the service must not have container_name configured; instead, let Compose automatically generate names (format like project-service-1, project-service-2).

Port mapping conflicts. If the service publishes a fixed host port (e.g., ports: - "8080:8080"), scaling multiple instances will cause port conflicts 
because all instances try to bind to host port 8080. To scale, you need to either remove the fixed port mapping or use a port range (e.g., "8080-8082:8080").

Practical Scenarios
In support work, you may encounter users who want to run multiple backend instances to test load balancing. The troubleshooting sequence is:
- Check if the service has container_name configured → if so, must remove it first.
- Check if fixed host port mapping is used → if so, scaling will cause port conflicts.
- Use docker compose up -d --scale <service>=<number> or docker compose scale <service>=<number>.
- Use docker compose ps to confirm the number of instances, container names should be in the form <project>-<service>-<number>.

In short, --scale is a tool for horizontally scaling service instances, but it is not compatible with fixed container names and fixed host port mappings.

= The docker compose port command (
This is a subcommand used to query an existing port mapping — it does not create or change anything. Usage :

* docker compose port [OPTIONS] SERVICE PRIVATE_PORT # It tells you which host port is mapped to a given container port for a service .

Options:
Option	                               Default	Description
--index		                  Container index when the service has multiple replicas e.g using --scale to create multiple same containers, queries them by their index no.
--protocol	tcp	            tcp or udp 
Why it matters for support: When a service uses a dynamic host port (e.g., ports: - "80" with no host side specified), Docker assigns a random host port on every up.
You can't guess it from the YAML — you must query it :

* docker compose port web 80
# Output: 0.0.0.0:55432
With --scale, each replica gets its own host port, so you need --index to query a specific one :
* docker compose port --index=1 web 80

